From: OpenIPC
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt7601sta: stop zeroing the middle of the BSSID in wireless events

RtmpOSWrielessEventSend() stores the address in wrqu.ap_addr.sa_data and then
unconditionally writes wrqu.data.length. The two are members of the same union, and
struct iw_point is { void *pointer; __u16 length; __u16 flags; } -- 4-byte pointer on
this ARM target -- so length lands at offset 4 and flags at offset 6, which is
sa_data[2] and sa_data[4] respectively.

The visible effect was an address with its middle two bytes missing. The CGIWAP event
for a completed association delivered 4c:22:00:00:23:9d for an AP whose BSSID is
4c:22:f3:74:23:9d (the first and last pairs survive: offsets 2 and 6 are only touched
for flags, which is -1 on that call). wpa_supplicant logged "new AP:
4c:22:00:00:23:9d", failed to match the AP against any configured network, and
deauthenticated with reason 3 (DEAUTH_LEAVING), which the AP reported as a 4-way
handshake timeout (Reason=15).

Fill in either the address or the data, never both.

Reported-by: boot log in devices/gm8135s_lite_beewi-bc7pw/NOTES.md
--- a/os/linux/rt_linux.c
+++ b/os/linux/rt_linux.c
@@ -1500,16 +1500,27 @@
 
 	memset(&wrqu, 0, sizeof (wrqu));
 
-	if (flags > -1)
-		wrqu.data.flags = flags;
-
 	if (pSrcMac)
+	{
+		/* An address and a data payload are separate members of the same
+		 * union, and struct iw_point puts length at offset 4 and flags at
+		 * offset 6 -- on top of sa_data[2..5]. Writing the data fields for
+		 * an address event therefore corrupts the address, and the
+		 * length = 0 below zeroed the middle two bytes of every BSSID that
+		 * reached wpa_supplicant: it received 4c:22:00:00:23:9d instead of
+		 * 4c:22:f3:74:23:9d, could not match the AP to a network
+		 * configuration, and deauthenticated (Reason=3, DEAUTH_LEAVING).
+		 */
 		memcpy(wrqu.ap_addr.sa_data, pSrcMac, MAC_ADDR_LEN);
-
-	if ((pData != NULL) && (dataLen > 0))
-		wrqu.data.length = dataLen;
+	}
 	else
-		wrqu.data.length = 0;
+	{
+		if (flags > -1)
+			wrqu.data.flags = flags;
+
+		if ((pData != NULL) && (dataLen > 0))
+			wrqu.data.length = dataLen;
+	}
 
 	wireless_send_event(pNetDev, eventType, &wrqu, (char *)pData);
 	return 0;
